    "q-129662": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129662",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}9-{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-3,3]\\times [-1,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-4,4]\\times [-1,1]$\n\nIII. $R=[-3,3]\\times [0,2]$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14257924/assets/4a8671db243a19fb258f6144d6aba1b193e237ef93d45d877829f80b93bc7d36.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}9-{x}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
